        <p>This guide covers the major producing countries, explains why production concentrates where it does, and shows you how to turn origin information into a sourcing decision. The trade-offs that decide most programs are fabric availability, print capability, lead time, and risk.</p>

        <h2 id="global-map">Where Are T-Shirts Manufactured? The Global Map at a Glance</h2>

        <p>Production clusters in three broad regions: Asia, Europe, and the Americas. Asia carries the volume. According to the WTO's <em>World Trade Statistical Review</em> (latest edition), China ranks first in apparel exports by value, with Bangladesh, Vietnam, and India also among the largest exporters. Europe contributes high-value near-shore capacity through Turkey and Portugal. The Americas serve regional demand from the United States, Mexico, and Central America.</p>

        <p>Here is the part most guides skip. A single T-shirt is rarely made in one place. Cotton may be grown in the US or India, spun into yarn in Vietnam, knitted into fabric in China, cut and sewn in Bangladesh, then printed in the destination country to shorten replenishment cycles. When people ask where T-shirts are made, they mean the cut-and-sew stage. Everything upstream of that stage crosses several borders.</p>

        <p>That distinction has real consequences. A "Made in Vietnam" label tells you where final assembly happened. It says nothing about where the fabric came from, who controls quality, or how fast a reorder can move. Those answers come from the factory, not the flag on the label.</p>

