        <h2 id="countries">The Top T-Shirt Manufacturing Countries and What Each Does Best</h2>

        <h3>China: The Full-Supply-Chain Hub</h3>

        <p>China has the deepest vertical integration in T-shirt production among major exporters. Inside a few hundred kilometers you can line up greige fabric, dyeing, cut-and-sew, screen printing, embroidery, and packaging. That density explains the range: 240 GSM oversized tees, garment-dyed vintage washes, moisture-wicking performance knits, multi-panel constructions with piping and contrast binding.</p>

        <p>For buyers, the payoff is optionality. Need a 320 GSM French terry crewneck tee, an enzyme wash, or puff print layered over discharge? China usually has it in-house or one district away. The trade-off shows up on plain goods: for a 30,000-piece run of 180 GSM cotton crews, South Asia will beat Chinese FOB pricing.</p>

        <h3>Vietnam: The Diversification Default</h3>

        <p>Vietnam has become the first alternative most brands test when they move volume out of China. Trade agreements pulled investment in, and the sector has held quality steady in both knits and wovens. T-shirt work here skews mid-volume: solid basics, 160–200 GSM combed cotton, clean two-to-four-color screen printing.</p>

        <p>Reliability is the selling point. Factory organization is generally tight and QC systems have matured alongside the export base. Specialty fabric is a weak spot, since much of it arrives imported, which adds two to three weeks and cost.</p>

        <h3>Bangladesh: Volume Economics</h3>

        <p>Bangladesh is a scale play. Cotton knits are the core competency, and T-shirts sit at the center of that. If you need 20,000 pieces of a 180 GSM single jersey tee at the lowest workable FOB, this is the obvious answer.</p>

        <p>Flexibility is where it gives ground. Short runs get deprioritized, complex decoration often goes outside the factory, and lead times stretch during the pre-holiday crush from roughly August through October. Bangladesh works on a 12–16 week planning horizon; below that, capacity allocation goes to larger accounts.</p>

        <h3>India: Cotton Depth and Finishing Craft</h3>

        <p>India pairs domestic cotton supply with a textile base that goes back generations. The range runs from commodity knits to GOTS-certified organic cotton, slub yarns, and hand-finished detail. Brands building around natural fibers, certification claims, or smaller premium batches find options here that Vietnam and Bangladesh cannot match.</p>

        <p>Export logistics are the variable. Port handling and inland transport swing more than in China or Vietnam, so build a two-week buffer into any Indian schedule.</p>

        <h3>Turkey and Portugal: Europe's Near-Shore Pair</h3>

        <p>Both serve EU brands on a compressed clock. Turkey is strong in cotton knits and can turn a repeat order in three to five weeks with truck freight into Western Europe in under a week. Portugal specializes in premium construction, garment dyeing, and runs of 300 to 1,000 pieces that Asian factories decline.</p>

        <p>The math for an EU brand is straightforward: a higher unit price buys back several weeks of calendar, which matters most for drop-driven businesses and mid-season restocks.</p>

        <h3>USA and Mexico: Speed and Labeling</h3>

        <p>US production exists to support "Made in USA" positioning and short runs at premium retail. Mexico and Central America sit in between, offering competitive labor for mid-volume programs with two to seven days of transit into US warehouses instead of a month at sea.</p>

        <p>This region fits programs where speed to shelf or a domestic label earns back the unit-cost gap; on pure scale economics, Asian FOB pricing still wins.</p>

