        <h2 id="why-they-are-made">Why T-Shirts Are Made Where They Are: Cost, Cotton, Capability</h2>

        <p>Three forces set the map.</p>

        <p><strong>Labor cost.</strong> Cut-and-sew resists automation, so assembly migrates toward lower wage structures. That single factor built the Bangladeshi and Vietnamese garment sectors.</p>

        <p><strong>Raw material access.</strong> India and China grow cotton and spin yarn at home. Bangladesh imports most of its fabric, which is why its cost edge lives in assembly rather than materials, and why fabric lead time is its recurring bottleneck.</p>

        <p><strong>Supply chain maturity.</strong> Efficient production needs mills, dye houses, trim suppliers, accessory makers, and freight infrastructure within reach of each other. China's density across those supporting industries is exactly why it absorbs the builds that require five specialized vendors to cooperate on one style.</p>

        <h2 id="origin-effects">How Country of Origin Affects Fabric, Quality, Lead Time and Risk</h2>

        <p>Origin shapes four things you will feel directly in your order.</p>

        <p><strong>Fabric availability.</strong> China and India offer the widest menus, from 120 GSM lightweight jersey through 300 GSM fleece-backed heavyweight. Vietnam and Bangladesh concentrate in the 160–220 GSM standard band. When a design depends on a specific hand-feel or finish, fabric access often decides your country before price gets a vote.</p>

        <p><strong>Quality consistency.</strong> Mature export sectors in China, Vietnam, and Portugal run standardized QC with documented AQL sampling. Newer clusters can produce strong work, but lot-to-lot variance widens. Ask any factory how in-line inspection is staffed and what the final audit report actually contains.</p>

        <p><strong>Lead time.</strong> Near-shore production in Turkey, Portugal, or Mexico removes three to five weeks of ocean freight, which is often worth a higher FOB for brands on a fixed drop calendar.</p>

        <p><strong>Risk.</strong> Long chains carry more exposure to port congestion, currency movement, tariff changes, and peak-season capacity reallocation; splitting volume across two regions hedges that at the cost of duplicated QC and two fit standards to reconcile.</p>

        <p>For how fabric choice interacts with origin, see our guide to <a href="/t-shirt-manufacturer/#tshirt-fabrics">fabric options for t-shirts</a>.</p>

        <p>Unsure which origin fits your design? <a href="/contact-us/">Talk to our team</a> and we will map your brief to a production setup.</p>

        <h2 id="choosing-a-factory">How to Tell If a Manufacturing Location (and Factory) Is Right for You</h2>

        <p>Origin is a filter, not a decision. The factory behind the label determines whether your order ships on time and to spec.</p>

        <h3>Direct Factory vs. Trading Company</h3>

        <p>A trading company sources on your behalf, usually across several factories. That helps when you need a broad product range and one point of contact. What you give up is visibility: you may never learn which factory ran your order, and QC sits one layer away from you.</p>

        <p>A direct factory owns its production lines. You see the floor, the QC process, and the people accountable when a measurement drifts. For brands building a recurring product line, a <a href="/t-shirt-manufacturer/">custom t-shirt manufacturer</a> with in-house capability delivers better consistency and solves problems faster.</p>

        <p>Match the model to the order. One-off promotional runs work fine through a trading partner. Recurring private-label programs belong with a direct factory.</p>

        <h3>First-Time Buyer Checklist: 8 Things to Verify Before You Order</h3>

        <ol class="ar-list"><li><strong>Production model</strong> — Factory or middleman? Ask for a live video walkthrough of the floor.</li><li><strong>Sampling capability</strong> — Can they build a pre-production sample in your actual fabric with your actual decoration?</li><li><strong>Fabric sourcing</strong> — Do they stock your target GSM and blend, or import it?</li><li><strong>Decoration range</strong> — Screen print, heat transfer, embroidery, sublimation: which run in-house?</li><li><strong>QC process</strong> — What checkpoints exist, and do you receive the inspection reports?</li><li><strong>Communication</strong> — Who is your named contact, and what response time do they commit to?</li><li><strong>Reorder capacity</strong> — Can they repeat the order without re-tooling or re-sampling?</li><li><strong>References</strong> — Ask for comparable programs they have already run.</li></ol>

        <p>A supplier who answers all eight without hedging has earned a sample order. One who dodges on production model or QC is a risk no country of origin will offset.</p>

        <h2 id="inside-a-factory">Inside a Modern T-Shirt Factory: From Sampling to Shipping</h2>

        <p>Here is how a custom T-shirt program runs at YAUUM, brief to delivery.</p>

        <h3>Sampling, Fabric, Cutting, Sewing, Printing and QC</h3>

        <p><strong>Sampling.</strong> Work starts from your tech pack or reference garment. A pre-production sample locks fit, fabric hand-feel, and decoration placement before bulk cutting begins. Large or multi-color prints get a strike-off first so color lands where you expect. Our <a href="/production/">t-shirt sampling process</a> walks through each stage.</p>

        <p><strong>Fabric sourcing.</strong> Fabric is matched to your GSM, blend, and finish spec, then tested for shrinkage and color fastness before anything reaches the cutting table.</p>

        <p><strong>Cutting.</strong> Patterns are graded across the size run and fabric is cut in layered stacks. Cutting accuracy is what keeps fit consistent from the first piece to the last.</p>

        <p><strong>Sewing.</strong> Assembly runs on dedicated lines with in-line checks that catch stitch defects and measurement drift before garments reach finishing.</p>

        <p><strong>Printing and finishing.</strong> Screen printing, heat transfer, embroidery, and specialty washes are applied to spec. Our <a href="/our-services/">custom t-shirt printing services</a> cover flat and dimensional decoration.</p>

        <h3>Quality Control Checkpoints at Every Stage</h3>

        <p>QC is not one inspection at the end. Fabric is inspected on receipt, garments are checked in-line during sewing, and a final audit covers measurements, stitching, decoration placement, and labeling before packing. Nothing that fails the audit goes into a carton.</p>

